/* ---- Section B: classification of taxes (Ch.6 paper pages 3-14, key p.57) ---- */
const gd=(...a)=>({c:a});
Qs("B1","B","HKCEE 1997-II Q.40",3,`Taxable income $10,000, $20,000, $30,000, $40,000 with tax payment $4,000, $7,000, $9,000, $10,000. The above tax is a …`,[`progressive tax`,`proportional tax`,`regressive tax`,`lump-sum tax`],"C",T`Average rates: @@4000/10000=40\%@@, @@35\%@@, @@30\%@@, @@25\%@@. The rate falls as income rises: regressive.`);
Qs("B2","B","HKCEE 1998-II Q.39",3,`Which of the following statements about tax is CORRECT?`,[`Progressive tax has a higher tax rate than regressive tax.`,`Progressive tax is a tax on income while regressive tax is a tax on goods.`,`The tax payment of a regressive tax decreases as the taxable income increases.`,`The percentage of income as tax of a progressive tax increases when taxable income increases.`],"D",`Progressive means the <b>percentage</b> paid rises with income. A is false (no comparison of levels), B mixes tax type with base, C is false (a regressive tax payment may still rise, only the rate falls).`);
Qs("B3","B","HKCEE 1999-II Q.40",4,`The diagram shows a tax payment curve that is concave: it rises quickly at first then flattens. Which statement is CORRECT?`,[`It is a progressive tax as the tax payment increases when income increases.`,`It would lead to an uneven distribution of income.`,`It would encourage the working incentive of workers.`,`The progressivity of the tax rate will increase when income rises.`],"B",`A tax payment curve that flattens means the tax rate (payment divided by income) falls: regressive, so the rich pay a lower proportion and the distribution becomes less even. A confuses a rising payment with a rising rate.`,gd(`Tax payment against income: a concave curve starting at the origin and flattening. See the page.`));
Qs("B4","B","HKCEE 2000-II Q.41",4,`Which of the following diagrams (tax payment against taxable income) illustrate a regressive tax measured in terms of an average tax rate? (1) a horizontal line, (2) a straight line through the origin, (3) a concave curve, (4) a convex curve getting steeper.`,[`(1) and (3) only`,`(1) and (4) only`,`(2) and (3) only`,`(2) and (4) only`],"A",`Average rate = payment / income. (1) a fixed payment: the rate falls. (3) concave: the rate falls. (2) a straight line through the origin: constant rate, proportional. (4) convex: rate rises, progressive.`,gd(`Four small graphs of tax payment against taxable income (1) to (4) as described. See the page.`));
Qs("B5","B","HKCEE 2000-II Q.43",5,`Taxable income $200,000, $250,000, $300,000 with tax payment $22,000, $25,000, $27,000. From the information we can conclude that …`,[`the tax is a progressive tax as the tax payment increases with taxable income.`,`the tax payment takes a decreasing portion of taxable income as taxable income increases.`,`the taxation system helps to reduce the income gap.`,`the taxation system reduces the work incentive.`],"B",T`Rates: @@22/200=11\%@@, @@25/250=10\%@@, @@27/300=9\%@@: falling, so regressive. A regressive tax does not narrow the income gap.`);
Qs("B6","B","HKCEE 2001-II Q.36",5,`Taxable income $10,000, $20,000, $30,000. Tax A payments $1,500, $3,800, $6,600; Tax B payments $2,000, $3,800, $5,400. From the data we can conclude that …`,[`Tax A is a direct tax and Tax B is an indirect tax.`,`there would be a more even income distribution under Tax A.`,`the tax rate of Tax A is lower than that of Tax B for all levels of income.`,`the fluctuations of government tax revenue would be smaller under Tax A than under Tax B when income fluctuates.`],"B",T`Tax A rates: 15%, 19%, 22% (progressive). Tax B rates: 20%, 19%, 18% (regressive). The progressive tax narrows the gap: B. C fails at $10,000 (15% vs 20%). D is reversed: a progressive tax makes revenue fluctuate more. (Your handwritten working on the scan gives the same rates.)`);
Qs("B7","B","HKCEE 2002-II Q.43",5,`Tobacco duty in Hong Kong is a kind of …`,[`direct and proportional tax.`,`direct and regressive tax.`,`indirect and proportional tax.`,`indirect and regressive tax.`],"D",`The seller can shift it to buyers (indirect). It is a fixed amount per quantity, so it takes a larger share of a poor smoker's income than of a rich one's: regressive.`);
Qs("B8","B","HKCEE 2003-II Q.38",6,`Which of the following taxes are progressive? (1) tax rate rising, convex curve; (2) tax rate rising, concave curve; (3) tax payment a straight line through the origin; (4) tax payment a horizontal line.`,[`(1) and (2) only`,`(1) and (3) only`,`(2) and (4) only`,`(3) and (4) only`],"A",`Progressive means the <b>tax rate</b> rises with income: (1) and (2). (3) is proportional (constant rate); (4) is a lump sum, regressive.`,gd(`Four graphs: (1) and (2) are tax RATE curves that rise; (3) and (4) are tax PAYMENT lines. See the page.`));
Qs("B9","B","HKCEE 2003-II Q.41",6,`Taxable income $10,000 to $50,000 (step $10,000) with tax payment $2,000, $3,800, $5,400, $6,800, $8,000. The above tax would …`,[`lead to a less even distribution of income.`,`reduce the working incentive of workers.`,`improve the resource allocation of the economy.`,`increase the consumption expenditure of the lower income group.`],"A",T`Rates: 20%, 19%, 18%, 17%, 16%: regressive, so the poor pay a larger share and the distribution becomes less even.`);
Qs("B10","B","HKCEE 2004-II Q.41",7,`Taxable income $10,000, $20,000, $30,000. Tax A: $500, $1,000, $1,500. Tax B: $800, $1,700, $2,700. Tax C: $1,000, $1,000, $1,000. Which statement is CORRECT?`,[`None of the above tax is a regressive tax.`,`The taxation principle of equity put forth by Adam Smith applies to all the above taxes.`,`Tax A is more effective in achieving more even income distribution.`,`Tax B is a progressive tax.`],"D",T`Tax A: 5%, 5%, 5% (proportional). Tax B: 8%, 8.5%, 9% (progressive). Tax C: lump sum, regressive (so A is false). Only the proportional tax satisfies equity (B false). A proportional tax does not narrow the gap (C false).`);
Qs("B11","B","HKCEE 2004-II Q.42",7,`Which line A, B, C or D shows the relationship between the tax rate and the taxable income under the profits tax system in Hong Kong?`,[`line A`,`line B`,`line C`,`line D`],"D",`Profits tax is charged at a flat rate on profits: the tax rate is horizontal. (A is a rising convex curve, B a rising straight line, C a rising concave curve, D a horizontal line.)`,gd(`Tax rate against taxable income, four lines A to D; D is horizontal. See the page.`));
Qs("B12","B","HKCEE 2005-II Q.39",7,`Taxable income $0, $10,000, $20,000 and tax $0, $1,500, X. If the above tax is progressive, the value of X must be …`,[`smaller than 3,000.`,`equal to 3,000.`,`greater than 3,000.`,`There is not sufficient information to do the calculation.`],"C",T`At $10,000 the rate is 15%. A progressive tax needs a higher rate at $20,000, so @@X>0.15\times20000=3000@@.`);
Qs("B13","B","HKCEE 2005-II Q.40",8,`Which of the following is/are the difference(s) between a direct tax and an indirect tax?`,[`The tax burden of a direct tax cannot be shifted onto other people, but the tax burden of an indirect tax can.`,`A direct tax results in a more even income distribution, but an indirect tax results in a less even income distribution.`,`The tax base of direct taxes is narrower, but the tax base of indirect taxes is broader.`,`All of the above are correct.`],"A",`The defining difference is whether the burden can be shifted. B and C are tendencies, not defining differences (a direct tax can be regressive, and so on).`);
Qs("B14","B","HKCEE 2005-II Q.43",8,`The tax payment against taxable income is a straight line through the origin. Which is CORRECT?`,[`The above tax would lower the working incentives of workers.`,`The above tax is in accordance with the equality principle put forth by Adam Smith.`,`The tax allowance for the above tax is zero.`,`Every employee has to pay the above tax.`],"B",`A straight line through the origin means a constant tax rate, a proportional tax, which is the equality principle. A flat rate does not by itself change work incentives (A), and a line from the origin does not tell you about allowances or who pays (C, D).`,gd(`Tax payment against taxable income: a straight line from the origin. See the page.`));
Qs("B15","B","HKCEE 2006-II Q.41",8,`Lines A, B, C and D show tax payment against taxable income (A convex and steep, B straight through the origin, C concave, D horizontal). Which line shows a relationship in accordance with the equality principle?`,[`line A`,`line B`,`line C`,`line D`],"B",`Equality means a proportional tax: payment grows in direct proportion to income, a straight line through the origin: line B.`,gd(`Four lines A to D of tax payment against taxable income. See the page.`));
Qs("B16","B","HKCEE 2008-II Q.43",9,`Which of the following diagrams describes a regressive tax? A: tax rate rising straight line. B: tax rate concave rising. C: tax payment straight line from the origin. D: tax payment concave flattening.`,[`A`,`B`,`C`,`D`],"D",`A flattening tax payment curve means a falling average rate: regressive. A and B show a rising tax <b>rate</b> (progressive); C is proportional.`,gd(`Four small graphs A to D, tax rate or tax payment against taxable income. See the page.`));
Qs("B17","B","HKCEE 2009-II Q.42",9,`An economy levies a 5% general sales tax on all goods and services. This tax is a/an …`,[`proportional tax as everyone will pay 5% of their expenditure on goods and services as tax.`,`progressive tax as the tax payment increases with the price of the goods and services.`,`regressive tax as the proportion of tax payment to income decreases when income increases.`,`indirect tax as consumers indirectly bear all the tax burden.`],"C",`The rich spend a smaller share of their income on goods and services, so tax paid as a share of <b>income</b> falls as income rises: regressive. (A is proportional to expenditure, not to income.)`);
Qs("B18","B","HKCEE 2009-II Q.43",10,`Tax rate rises along a straight line as taxable income rises. Which statement is CORRECT?`,[`The taxation principle of equity put forth by Adam Smith applies to this tax.`,`The tax rate remains constant when the taxable income increases.`,`The imposition of this tax would lead to an increase in the ratio of indirect tax revenue to direct tax revenue.`,`When the taxable income increases, the percentage increase of the tax payment is greater than the percentage increase of the taxable income.`],"D",T`A rising tax rate is progressive: @@\text{payment}=\text{rate}\times\text{income}@@, so payment grows faster than income. Equity needs a constant rate (A, B false).`,gd(`Tax rate against taxable income: a rising straight line. See the page.`));
Qs("B19","B","HKCEE 2010-II Q.41",10,`Taxable income $100,000, $200,000, $300,000. Tax system A: $4,000 each. Tax system B: $5,000, $10,000, $15,000. Tax system A is ______ while tax system B is ______.`,[`proportional ... progressive`,`progressive ... proportional`,`regressive ... proportional`,`regressive ... progressive`],"C",T`A is a lump sum (4%, 2%, 1.3%): regressive. B is 5%, 5%, 5%: proportional.`);
Qs("B20","B","HKDSE Practice Paper 1 Q.36",10,`Which of the following is a direct and proportional tax in Hong Kong?`,[`hotel accommodation tax`,`profits tax`,`salaries tax`,`general rates`],"B",`Profits tax is levied on the company and has a flat rate. Salaries tax is progressive; rates are indirect; the hotel tax was indirect.`);
Qs("B21","B","HKDSE 2014-I Q.36",11,`The government plans a new tax under which the tax payment drops when the taxable income drops. Which statement is CORRECT?`,[`The tax is regressive as the tax rate increases when the taxable income drops.`,`The tax is proportional as the taxable income and the tax payment are positively related.`,`The tax cannot be progressive as the tax rate increases when the tax payment drops.`,`The tax can be progressive, proportional or regressive.`],"D",`A positive link between income and payment is true of all three kinds; it does not identify the type. You need the tax rate.`);
Qs("B22","B","HKDSE 2017-I Q.35",11,`Which of the following diagrams describes a regressive tax? (1) tax payment concave, (2) tax payment horizontal, (3) tax rate rising straight line, (4) tax rate concave rising.`,[`(1) and (2) only`,`(1) and (3) only`,`(2) and (4) only`,`(3) and (4) only`],"A",`(1) a flattening payment curve and (2) a fixed payment both mean a falling rate: regressive. (3) and (4) have a rising rate: progressive.`,gd(`Four graphs, the first two are tax payment, the last two are tax rate. See the page.`));
Qs("B23","B","HKDSE 2019-I Q.36",12,`The tax payment is a horizontal line against taxable income. Which statement is CORRECT?`,[`The lower income group pays a smaller percentage of their taxable income as tax payment.`,`It violates the tax principle of equity put forward by Adam Smith.`,`It is a proportional tax.`,`It leads to a more even income distribution.`],"B",`A lump sum is regressive: the lower income group pays a <b>larger</b> percentage (A false), so it violates equity (constant rate), is not proportional and widens the gap.`,gd(`Tax payment against taxable income: a horizontal line. See the page.`));

const LONG_B=[
{id:"LB1",src:`HKCEE 1998-I Q.5 (printed answer p.57)`,p:12,pr:"12",f:"s6",k:["s6",57,"57"],q:`Three income tax proposals. Proposal A: the tax payment curve is concave (flattens). Proposal B: the tax payment curve is convex (gets steeper). Proposal C: a straight line through the origin. Explain the differences in terms of tax payment against taxable income, and say which would be most effective in achieving a more even distribution of income. (7 marks)`,m:`<p><b>A:</b> the tax payment takes a <b>decreasing</b> proportion of taxable income as taxable income rises (regressive). <b>B:</b> an <b>increasing</b> proportion (progressive). <b>C:</b> the <b>same</b> proportion (proportional).</p><p>Under proposal B, people with high income pay a larger proportion of their income as tax and those with lower income pay less, so it can reduce the income disparity and is the most effective.</p>`},
{id:"LB2",src:`HKCEE 1999-I Q.8 (answer p.57 and p.59)`,p:13,pr:"13",f:"s6",k:["s6",57,"57"],q:`Taxable income $10,000, $20,000, $30,000 with tax amount $1,000, $1,900, $2,700. Explain whether the tax is progressive. Show your working. (5 marks)`,m:T`<table><tr><th>Taxable income</th><th>$10,000</th><th>$20,000</th><th>$30,000</th></tr><tr><td>Tax</td><td class="n">$1,000</td><td class="n">$1,900</td><td class="n">$2,700</td></tr><tr><td>Average tax rate</td><td class="n">10%</td><td class="n">9.5%</td><td class="n">9%</td></tr></table><p><b>No.</b> The average tax rate does not increase as taxable income rises; it decreases, so the tax is regressive.</p>`},
{id:"LB3",src:`HKCEE 2000-I Q.9a (printed answer p.58)`,p:13,pr:"13",f:"s6",k:["s6",58,"58"],q:`A gasoline bill: 50 litres, price per litre $3.78 (= $189), government tax $6.06 per litre (= $303), total $492. Define regressive tax and explain whether the gasoline tax is progressive, proportional or regressive. (5 marks)`,m:`<p>A regressive tax is one whose tax rate <b>decreases</b> as income increases. Consumers pay the same tax for the same amount of gasoline. In proportion to income the richer pay at a lower rate and the poorer at a higher rate, so the gasoline tax is regressive.</p>`},
{id:"LB4",src:`HKCEE 2001-I Q.7a (printed answer p.58)`,p:13,pr:"13",f:"s6",k:["s6",58,"58"],q:`Explain whether a general sales tax is a direct tax or an indirect tax. (2 marks)`,m:`<p>An <b>indirect</b> tax, because the tax burden can be shifted from sellers of goods and services onto buyers.</p>`},
{id:"LB5",src:`HKCEE 2002-I Q.12c (printed answer p.58)`,p:13,pr:"13",f:"s6",k:["s6",58,"58"],q:`Mr. Wong's bookstore pays profits tax and rates. (i) Explain which is an indirect tax. (2) (ii) Explain whether profits tax is progressive, proportional or regressive. (3)`,m:`<p>(i) <b>Rates</b> are indirect because the burden can be shifted from landlords onto tenants. (ii) The profits tax is <b>proportional</b> because the effective tax rate is fixed regardless of the amount of firms' profits.</p>`},
{id:"LB6",src:`HKCEE 2003-I Q.10d (printed answer p.58)`,p:13,pr:"13",f:"s6",k:["s6",58,"58"],q:`The first registration tax on a newly imported car is a fixed percentage of the car's value. Explain whether the tax is proportional. (3 marks)`,m:`<p>It is <b>not</b> proportional to people's income, since the tax rate (relative to income) decreases as the income of car buyers increases. It is a fixed percentage of the car's value, not of income.</p>`},
{id:"LB7",src:`HKCEE 2004-I Q.10e (printed answer p.58)`,p:13,pr:"13",f:"s6",k:["s6",58,"58"],q:`The air passenger departure tax is $120 per adult passenger. Explain whether the tax is progressive, proportional or regressive. (3 marks)`,m:`<p><b>Regressive:</b> the payment is fixed at $120 for people with different incomes. As income increases the tax rate decreases.</p>`},
{id:"LB9",src:`HKCEE 2008-I Q.8 (printed answer p.58)`,p:14,pr:"14",f:"s6",k:["s6",58,"58"],q:`Red wine is subject to an excise tax of 40% of its value. (a) Explain whether this tax is progressive, regressive or proportional. (4) (b) Explain whether it is a direct or indirect tax. (2)`,m:`<p>(a) <b>Regressive</b>: the tax payment on the same wine is fixed for buyers with different incomes; as income rises the rate falls. (b) <b>Indirect</b>: the burden can be shifted from red wine sellers onto consumers.</p>`},
{id:"LB12",src:`HKDSE 2019-II Q.10a (printed answer p.59)`,p:14,pr:"14",f:"s6",k:["s6",59,"59"],q:`China's individual income tax brackets (monthly, RMB): average tax rate 3%, 10%, 20%, 25%, 30%, 35%, 45% for increasing ranges of taxable income. Is the tax progressive, proportional or regressive? Explain. (2 marks)`,m:`<p><b>Progressive</b>, because as taxable income increases, the average tax rate also increases (3% up to 45%).</p>`}
];

/* ---- Section D: effects of fiscal policy (Ch.6 paper pages 21-39, key p.59) ---- */
Qs("D1","D","HKCEE 1998-II Q.37",21,`A progressive tax is more effective than a proportional tax in achieving the following aim:`,[`to increase government revenue`,`to lower the administrative costs of the government`,`to achieve a more equitable income distribution`,`to reallocate the resources of the economy`],"C",`A progressive tax takes a larger share from the rich, so the income distribution becomes more equal.`);
Qs("D2","D","HKCEE 1998-II Q.38",21,`When the profits tax rate decreases,`,[`the government revenue will decrease.`,`the government expenditure will decrease.`,`the incentive of investment will increase.`,`the unemployment rate will increase.`],"C",`After-tax profit per dollar of investment rises, so the incentive to invest rises. Revenue need not fall (A): more investment can widen profits.`);
Qs("D3","D","HKCEE 1998-II Q.41",22,`Suppose the sales tax rate of a good is raised. Which statement is CORRECT?`,[`The total expenditure on the good will increase.`,`There will be inflation.`,`The total revenue net of tax to sellers will fall if the supply curve is upward sloping.`,`Income will be redistributed from the poor to the rich.`],"C",`The tax shifts supply left: sellers get a lower net price and sell fewer units, so net revenue falls. Total expenditure may rise or fall (A); a one-off rise in price is not inflation (B); the effect on income distribution is toward the rich only in a limited sense (D).`);
Qs("D4","D","HKCEE 1999-II Q.43",22,`Which of the following government policies would lead to a more even distribution of income?`,[`lowering the tax rates of regressive taxes`,`imposing more indirect taxes on necessities`,`increasing the depreciation allowance of the profits tax`,`reducing the standard tax rate of the salaries tax`],"A",`Lowering a regressive tax cuts the burden of the poor, who pay a larger share. B hurts the poor; C and D mainly benefit firms and high earners.`);
Qs("D5","D","HKCEE 2000-II Q.38",22,`Which of the following would MOST likely increase employment during an economic downturn?`,[`restructuring of private firms to improve their efficiency`,`increasing computerization and automation of production process`,`government's injection of a large amount of funds to help small enterprises`,`increasing real interest rates`],"C",`Funds to small firms support output and hiring. A and B tend to cut jobs; D raises the cost of borrowing.`);
Qs("D6","D","HKCEE 2001-II Q.35",22,`A graph shows tax payment against income before (old) and after (new) a change in a tax's structure: the new curve starts at a higher income and rises more steeply then flattens above the old line. Compared to the old tax structure, the new tax structure …`,[`results in a less even income distribution.`,`has a lower tax rate for the same level of income.`,`has a larger tax allowance.`,`has no effect on working incentive.`],"A",`The new curve is above the old at every income, so tax rates are higher for everyone and flatten at high incomes (more regressive), giving a less even distribution. (The new curve begins slightly to the right of the old one at low incomes in the picture, which is a feature of the diagram.)`,{c:[`Tax payment against income with two curves: the new structure is concave and above the old. See the page.`]});
Qs("D7","D","HKCEE 2001-II Q.39",23,`Which of the following will widen the tax base of Hong Kong?`,[`an increase in the tax allowance`,`a decrease in the standard tax rate`,`an increase in the profits tax rate`,`the introduction of a sales tax`],"D",`A new type of tax puts goods and services (and everyone who buys them) into the tax net. A higher allowance would narrow the base; the rates do not change who is taxed.`);
Qs("D8","D","HKCEE 2002-II Q.40",23,`If a general sales tax is introduced in Hong Kong,`,[`the tax base would be widened.`,`the wealth gap would be reduced.`,`less Hong Kong people would shop in Shenzhen.`,`the proportion of direct tax in the government tax revenue would increase.`],"A",`It adds a new tax on all purchases. It is regressive (B false), would push shoppers to Shenzhen (C false), and raises the indirect share (D false).`);
Qs("D9","D","HKCEE 2002-II Q.42",23,`The government cuts the salaries tax allowance by 10% while both the wage rates and the general price level fall by 5%. It follows that`,[`the tax revenue would increase.`,`more people would fall into the tax net.`,`the real income of people before tax would generally increase.`,`the ratio of people's tax payment to their nominal income would generally decrease.`],"B",`A lower allowance and lower wages both ... the allowance falls by 10% while wages fall only 5%, so more people have income above the allowance and enter the tax net. Real income before tax is unchanged (wages and prices fall equally).`);
Qs("D10","D","HKCEE 2003-II Q.31",23,`In times of economic recession, the Hong Kong government reduces the unemployment allowance and imposes a general sales tax in order to reduce the budget deficit. These changes would lead to`,[`an increase in the employment rate.`,`an increase in the inflation rate.`,`a wider wealth gap between the rich and the poor.`,`an increase in the fiscal reserves of the government.`],"C",`Both measures fall hardest on the poor (less transfer, a regressive tax), so the wealth gap widens. Employment and inflation would not rise; a deficit reduction need not raise reserves.`);
Qs("D11","D","HKCEE 2003-II Q.40",23,`If the government raises the profits tax rate,`,[`the government revenue will increase.`,`the unemployment rate will fall.`,`the incentive for investment will decrease.`,`the general price level will rise.`],"C",`A higher profits tax lowers after-tax profits, so the incentive to invest falls. Revenue is uncertain; unemployment is more likely to rise.`);
Qs("D12","D","HKCEE 2003-II Q.42",24,`Suppose the government of Hong Kong introduces a general sales tax. It follows that`,[`the sales revenue net of tax to the sellers will decrease.`,`there will be a persistent increase in the general price level.`,`income will be redistributed from the rich to the poor.`,`increase the consumption expenditure of the lower income group.`],"A",`Part of the price goes to the government, so sellers receive less per unit and sell fewer units. The price rise is one-off, the tax is regressive, and it lowers consumption.`);
Qs("D13","D","HKCEE 2004-II Q.43",24,`Marginal tax bands (first $30,000, next $30,000, next $30,000, remainder) change from rates 2.0%, 7.5%, 13.0%, 18.5% (old system) to 2.0%, 8.0%, 14.0%, 20.0% (new system). Under the new system,`,[`the ratio of direct tax revenue to indirect tax revenue will decrease.`,`the government will have a budget surplus.`,`more people will fall into the tax net.`,`the income gap will be reduced.`],"D",T`The upper-band rates rise (2.0 to 2.0, 7.5 to 8.0, 13.0 to 14.0, 18.5 to 20.0), so higher earners pay more: more progressive, narrowing the gap. Direct revenue rises relative to indirect (A false); no information about a surplus; the first band is unchanged so the net does not widen.`);
Qs("D14","D","HKCEE 2005-II Q.41",24,`Which of the following government policies would lead to a less even income distribution of Hong Kong citizens?`,[`raising the standard tax rate of the salaries tax`,`reducing the personal allowance of the salaries tax`,`raising the profits tax rate`,`lowering the excise tax rate of tobacco and wine`],"B",`A smaller allowance raises tax on lower earners proportionally more (more people enter the net, middle incomes pay more), widening the gap. A raises tax on the rich; D lowers a regressive tax.`);
Qs("D15","D","HKCEE 2005-II Q.42",24,`The introduction of a general sales tax would lead to`,[`inflation.`,`lower output.`,`higher consumption expenditure.`,`higher GDP.`],"B",`The tax raises the cost of production (SRAS shifts left) and cuts disposable income: output falls and the price level rises once. A one-off rise is not inflation.`);
Qs("D16","D","HKCEE 2006-II Q.39",25,`The salaries tax changes from system A (marginal rates 3%, 6%, 10%, 15% on four $20,000 bands) to system B (2%, 5%, 8%, 12%). As a result,`,[`less people would fall into the tax net.`,`the working incentive of workers would increase.`,`the income gap would be reduced.`,`the ratio of direct tax revenue to indirect tax revenue would increase.`],"B",`Every marginal rate falls, so workers keep more of each extra dollar: working incentive rises. The tax net is unchanged (the first band still starts at 0), the gap is not narrowed since all rates fall together, and direct revenue falls.`);
Qs("D17","D","HKCEE 2006-II Q.42",25,`Which of the following would widen the tax base of Hong Kong?`,[`a reduction in the tax allowance`,`abolition of the first registration tax on motor vehicles`,`an increase in the progressivity of salaries tax`,`an increase in the profits tax rate`],"A",`A lower allowance brings more people into the salaries tax net. Abolition narrows it; rates and progressivity do not change who is taxed.`);
Qs("D18","D","HKCEE 2007-II Q.42",25,`Taxable income $100,000, $200,000, $300,000. Tax system A: $6,000, $12,000, $18,000. Tax system B: $5,000, $12,000, $21,000. When the tax system changes from A to B,`,[`the income gap between the rich and the poor will be reduced.`,`the work incentive of workers will increase.`,`the tax base of the economy will be widened.`,`the government budget position will be improved.`],"A",T`A: 6%, 6%, 6% (proportional). B: 5%, 6%, 7% (progressive). The shift lowers the poor's burden and raises the rich's, narrowing the gap.`);
Qs("D19","D","HKCEE 2007-II Q.43",26,`Some businessmen propose to reduce the profits tax rate to increase competitiveness. When the profits tax rate decreases,`,[`the foreign exchange and gold reserve of Hong Kong would increase.`,`the investment incentive would increase.`,`the government tax revenue would decrease.`,`the size of the public sector would decrease.`],"B",`Higher after-tax profit raises the incentive to invest. Revenue effects are uncertain (C), and reserves do not obviously change (A).`);
Qs("D20","D","HKCEE 2008-II Q.41",26,`When the government reduces the profits tax rate,`,[`the unemployment rate will increase.`,`the investment incentive will increase.`,`the government tax revenue will decrease.`,`the income inequality will be reduced.`],"B",`Same logic as D19.`);
Qs("D21","D","HKCEE 2008-II Q.42",26,`The Government will launch 10 major infrastructure projects. This will result in (1) an increase in the GDP of Hong Kong. (2) an increase in the size of the public sector. (3) government budget deficits in the years of construction.`,[`(1) and (2) only`,`(1) and (3) only`,`(2) and (3) only`,`(1), (2) and (3)`],"A",`Spending is G, so GDP rises (1), and public expenditure rises relative to GDP in the construction years (2). A deficit (3) is not certain: it depends on revenue.`);
Qs("D22","D","HKCEE 2008-II Q.44",26,`The salaries tax bands widen from $30,000 to $40,000 each (rates 2%, 7%, 13%, 19%, standard rate 16%). Under the new system, with the salaries of all taxpayers unchanged,`,[`fewer people will fall into the tax net.`,`fewer people will have to pay at the standard rate.`,`the income gap will not be affected.`,`the government will run into a budget deficit.`],"B",`Wider bands mean more income is taxed at low rates, so fewer taxpayers reach the point where the progressive tax would exceed the 16% cap. The first band still starts at zero so the net is unchanged.`);
Qs("D23","D","HKCEE 2009-II Q.44",27,`In 2008-09 the standard rate of salaries tax was reduced from 16% to 15%. Other things being equal, the measure would`,[`reduce the number of people falling into the tax net.`,`increase the progressivity of the salaries tax system of Hong Kong.`,`increase the consumption expenditure of people in Hong Kong.`,`increase the after tax income of low-income people in Hong Kong.`],"C",`Only taxpayers at the standard rate benefit (high earners), raising their disposable income and so consumption. It does not change who is in the net, and low earners pay below the cap so are unaffected (D).`);
Qs("D24","D","HKCEE 2009-II Q.45",27,`An increase in the Hong Kong government's expenditure on infrastructure must lead to an increase in`,[`the ratio of public expenditure to GDP in Hong Kong.`,`the government budget deficits.`,`the wage rate of workers in Hong Kong.`,`the potential GDP of Hong Kong in the future.`],"D",`Infrastructure is capital: it raises productivity and the capital stock, so potential output rises in the future. The ratio, deficit and wages need not move (A, B, C are "may", not "must").`);
Qs("D25","D","HKCEE 2010-II Q.42",27,`There was a one-off tax reduction up to a maximum of $6,000 on the salaries tax for 2008-09. This would`,[`reduce the income gap between the rich and the poor.`,`narrow the tax base of Hong Kong.`,`increase the working incentive of those paying at the standard tax rate.`,`increase the consumption expenditure of those entitled to the tax reduction.`],"D",`A rebate raises disposable income of taxpayers, so their consumption rises. It benefits taxpayers, not non-payers (A false); a one-off change does not alter the base or the marginal rate (B, C false).`);
Qs("D26","D","HKCEE 2010-II Q.43",27,`During a recession, a government promotes growth through infrastructural development. As a result, (1) the size of the public sector would increase. (2) the employment opportunity would increase. (3) the government budget would run into a deficit.`,[`(1) and (2) only`,`(1) and (3) only`,`(2) and (3) only`,`(1), (2) and (3)`],"A",`More spending raises public expenditure relative to GDP (1) and creates jobs (2). A deficit (3) depends on the revenue side, so it is not certain.`);
Qs("D27","D","HKDSE Sample Paper 1 Q.36",27,`Which of the following can help to solve an inflationary gap?`,[`an increase in taxes`,`an increase in budget deficit`,`an increase in transfer payments`,`an increase in government expenditure`],"A",`An inflationary gap needs lower AD. Higher taxes cut disposable income and consumption. B, C, D all raise AD.`);
Qs("D28","D","HKDSE 2012-I Q.34",28,`The government spends $3 billion on a cruise terminal financed from its reserves. This will result in (1) an increase in aggregate output in the short run. (2) an increase in aggregate output in the long run. (3) an increase in employment in the short run. (4) a budget deficit in the years of construction.`,[`(1) and (3) only`,`(2) and (4) only`,`(1), (2) and (3) only`,`(1), (3) and (4) only`],"C",`G rises, so AD rises: output (1) and employment (3) rise in the short run. A terminal adds to the capital stock, so output in the long run also rises (2). The spending is financed from reserves, so there is no budget deficit (4 false).`);
Qs("D29","D","HKDSE 2013-I Q.23",28,`The aggregate demand curve will shift to the right if`,[`there is a fall in the price level.`,`there is an improvement in technology.`,`the government reduces the import tariff.`,`the government reduces the salaries tax rate.`],"D",`A lower salaries tax raises disposable income, so consumption and AD rise. A is a movement along AD; B shifts AS; C raises imports, lowering net exports and AD.`);
Qs("D30","D","HKDSE 2013-I Q.29",28,`The government greatly increases the tax allowance on continuing education. Which diagram can best explain the effect on the price and output levels?`,[`A: LRAS shifts right and AD shifts right`,`B: LRAS shifts left and AD shifts right`,`C: SRAS shifts right and AD shifts left`,`D: LRAS and SRAS shift right with AD unchanged`],"A",`The allowance raises disposable income (AD right) and, by encouraging training, raises human capital: LRAS shifts right in the future. (Described from the four diagrams on the page.)`,{c:[`Four AS-AD diagrams A to D. See the page.`]});
Qs("D31","D","HKDSE 2013-I Q.36",29,`A diagram shows the tax rate against income for the old and new salaries tax structures: the new tax structure's rising section starts at a higher income and rises to a higher flat rate. Compared to the old salaries tax structure, the new tax structure`,[`has a smaller personal allowance.`,`has a lower standard tax rate.`,`will narrow the income gap between the rich and the poor.`,`will widen the tax base.`],"C",`The new line starts at a higher income (a larger allowance, A false) and ends at a higher flat rate (B false). A higher standard rate and a larger allowance mean the rich pay more and the poor less, narrowing the gap. A larger allowance narrows the base (D false).`,{c:[`Tax rate against income, two lines: new tax structure is shifted right with a higher standard rate. See the page.`]});
Qs("D32","D","HKDSE 2014-I Q.28",29,`The Old Age Living Allowance introduced by the Hong Kong government increases the ______ in GDP and results in a ______ shift of the aggregate demand curve.`,[`government consumption expenditure ... rightward`,`government consumption expenditure ... leftward`,`private consumption expenditure ... rightward`,`private consumption expenditure ... leftward`],"C",`The allowance is a transfer payment, which is not government consumption. It raises recipients' disposable income, so private consumption rises and AD shifts right.`);
Qs("D33","D","HKDSE 2014-I Q.35",29,`Which of the following policies can the government introduce to reduce the price and unemployment levels at the same time?`,[`to raise the discount rates`,`to raise the subsidies to small and medium-sized enterprises`,`to raise the salaries tax rate`,`to raise the expenditure on social welfare`],"B",`A subsidy lowers the cost of production: SRAS shifts right, so the price falls and output (and employment) rises. A, C lower AD (price falls but output falls too); D raises AD.`);
Qs("D34","D","HKDSE 2014-I Q.38",30,`Which of the following government policies will lead to an increase in the aggregate output of a country in the long run? (1) to tighten regulations on environmental protection (2) to grant an amnesty to illegal immigrants (3) to ban the importation of foreign domestic helpers (4) to build more international schools for attracting expatriates to work in the country`,[`(1) and (3) only`,`(1) and (4) only`,`(2) and (3) only`,`(2) and (4) only`],"D",`Long-run output rises when labour supply or productivity rises. An amnesty (2) adds legal workers; schools (4) attract expatriates. Tighter rules (1) and banning helpers (3) cut resources or labour.`);
Qs("D35","D","HKDSE 2015-I Q.27",30,`The government provides cash subsidies for new-born children from their birth to age 10. Which diagram BEST illustrates the effect of the subsidies on the economy after twenty years?`,[`SRAS shifts right`,`AD shifts right`,`SRAS shifts left, AD shifts right`,`LRAS shifts right and AD shifts right`],"D",`After twenty years the children have grown into the labour force: the labour supply and LRAS rise, and extra population raises AD too. (Described from the four diagrams.)`,{c:[`Four AS-AD diagrams A to D. See the page.`]});
Qs("D36","D","HKDSE 2015-I Q.38",31,`Initially an economy operates at the long-run equilibrium. Which of the following will lead to a deflationary (output) gap in the economy?`,[`The government increases the salaries tax rate.`,`The government reduces the profits tax rate.`,`The government invests in the construction of a cross-border express rail link.`,`The central bank reduces the required reserve ratio.`],"A",`A deflationary gap needs AD to fall. A higher salaries tax lowers disposable income and consumption. B, C and D raise AD.`);
Qs("D37","D","HKDSE 2015-I Q.39",31,`In an economy with severe inflation and income inequality, the government can lower the price level and reduce the income gap by`,[`increasing the environmental tax on handling household sewage.`,`increasing the salaries tax and its progressivity.`,`reducing its spending on social welfare.`,`reducing the personal allowance of the salaries tax.`],"B",`A progressive salaries tax cuts AD (lowering the price level) and takes proportionally more from the rich (narrowing the gap). A is regressive; C hurts the poor; D hurts lower earners.`);
Qs("D38","D","HKDSE 2016-I Q.27",31,`If the government builds new infrastructures, there will be an increase in (1) employed population during the construction period. (2) the price level during the construction period. (3) potential output after the construction period. (4) the price level after the construction period.`,[`(1) and (2) only`,`(3) and (4) only`,`(1), (2) and (3) only`,`(1), (2), (3) and (4)`],"C",`Construction spending raises AD: employment (1) and the price level (2) rise. The infrastructure then raises LRAS: potential output rises (3). After construction, LRAS shifting right lowers the price level, so (4) is false.`);
Qs("D39","D","HKDSE 2016-I Q.36",32,`A diagram shows the changes in the salaries tax structure: old (dashed) has a higher standard rate and a lower start than the new (solid), which starts at a higher income and caps at a lower rate. Compared to the old tax structure, the new tax structure will ____.`,[`result in a more even income distribution`,`reduce the work incentive of all workers`,`broaden the tax base`,`lead to an increase in total tax revenue`],"C",`The new line starts at a lower income than the old dashed line in the picture, so more people fall into the net: the base is broadened. A lower standard rate benefits the rich (A false), raises the incentive (B false) and, with a lower cap, revenue need not rise (D).`,{c:[`Tax rate against income: a dashed old structure and a solid new one with a lower standard rate and lower starting income. See the page.`]});
Qs("D40","D","HKDSE 2017-I Q.37",32,`Which of the following policies would MOST likely lead to a drop in aggregate output?`,[`The government bought bonds from the public and raised the tax allowance at the same time.`,`The government sold bonds to the public and reduced transfer payment to the poor at the same time.`,`The government reduced required reserve ratio and raised the progressivity of income tax at the same time.`,`The government increased the discount rate and lowered profits tax rate at the same time.`],"B",`Selling bonds drains the money supply (interest rate up) and lower transfers cut disposable income: both lower AD. A raises AD; C and D each have one expansionary and one contractionary move.`);
Qs("D41","D","HKDSE 2017-I Q.39",33,`The economy initially operates at E1 (a deflationary gap, with imports equal to exports). If the government decreases the social security assistance to the elderly, in the short run, the deflationary (output) gap will be ______ and there will be a ______.`,[`narrower ... trade surplus`,`wider ... trade deficit`,`narrower ... trade deficit`,`wider ... trade surplus`],"D",`Lower transfers reduce consumption: AD falls, output falls further (a wider gap). Lower income reduces imports while exports are unchanged: a trade surplus.`,{c:[`AS-AD diagram with E1 to the left of LRAS (deflationary gap). See the page.`]});
Qs("D42","D","HKDSE 2017-I Q.40",34,`The Financial Secretary proposed to waive business registration fees for 2016-17 and raised the personal basic tax allowance from $120,000 to $132,000. Which BEST illustrates the effect on the price and output levels?`,[`LRAS shifts right and AD shifts left`,`AD shifts right`,`SRAS shifts left and AD shifts right`,`LRAS shifts right and SRAS shifts right with AD fixed`],"B",`The allowance raises disposable income, so AD shifts right. Waiving a fee changes the cost of production only slightly and for a year, so the printed answer shows only the AD shift. (Described from the four diagrams.)`,{c:[`Four AS-AD diagrams A to D. See the page.`]});
Qs("D43","D","HKDSE 2018-I Q.28",35,`The diagram shows AD curves, SRAS curves and LRAS of an economy that initially operates at E0 (with five other equilibria E1 to E4). If the government gives $10,000 cash to every citizen every year, the short-run equilibrium will be at ______ and the long-run equilibrium will be at ______.`,[`E2 ... E0`,`E2 ... E1`,`E3 ... E0`,`E3 ... E4`],"B",`The cash raises disposable income: AD shifts right, so the short-run equilibrium moves to E2 (higher price and output). In the long run the output gap is inflationary, SRAS shifts left, and the economy returns to LRAS at a higher price level: E1.`,{c:[`AS-AD diagram with points E0 to E4 and AD0, AD1, AD2, SRAS0, SRAS1, SRAS2. See the page.`]});
Qs("D44","D","HKDSE 2018-I Q.38",35,`A reduction in the basic allowance of the salaries tax lowers aggregate demand. Which of the following can offset its effect on aggregate demand?`,[`People's desire to save increases.`,`The price level decreases.`,`The central bank carries out an open market sale of government bonds.`,`The required reserve ratio in the banking system decreases.`],"D",`A lower required reserve ratio expands the money supply, lowers the interest rate and raises consumption and investment: AD rises. A and C lower AD; B is a movement along AD.`);
Qs("D45","D","HKDSE 2018-I Q.39",36,`The economy operates at E1 (above LRAS: an inflationary gap). Which of the following can help restore the long run equilibrium? (1) to reduce the subsidies to small enterprises (2) to increase the required reserve ratio (3) to reduce the discount rate (4) to reduce property tax`,[`(1) and (2) only`,`(3) and (4) only`,`(1), (2) and (3) only`,`(2), (3) and (4) only`],"A",`An inflationary gap needs AD down or SRAS left... fewer subsidies raise costs (SRAS left) and a higher RRR contracts money: both close the gap. (3) and (4) raise AD, widening it.`,{c:[`AS-AD diagram with E1 to the right of LRAS. See the page.`]});
Qs("D46","D","HKDSE 2019-I Q.34",36,`Both the wage rate and general price level increase by 8%. If the government increases the salaries tax allowance by 5%,`,[`more people will fall into the tax net.`,`income distribution will become more even.`,`government's tax revenue will decrease.`,`people's real income after tax will increase.`],"A",`Wages rise 8% but the allowance only 5%, so more people's incomes exceed the allowance and enter the net (tax revenue rises, so C false).`);
Qs("D47","D","HKDSE 2019-I Q.38",37,`Policy one: reduce the profits tax rate for corporations with profit less than two million dollars. Policy two: inject fifty billion dollars to develop innovation and technology. Which AD-AS diagram could MOST likely show the effect on aggregate output?`,[`SRAS shifts right and AD shifts left`,`SRAS shifts left and AD shifts right`,`LRAS shifts right and AD shifts right`,`LRAS shifts right with AD unchanged`],"C",`Policy one raises investment (AD right); policy two raises productivity and technology (LRAS right in the future). (Described from the four diagrams.)`,{c:[`Four AD-AS diagrams A to D. See the page.`]});
Qs("D48","D","HKDSE 2019-I Q.41",37,`Policy I: an open market purchase of government bonds and a rise in the discount rate. Policy II: an open market purchase and a rise in the salaries tax allowance. Policy III: a rise in the salaries tax allowance and a rise in the discount rate. Which policies are expansionary?`,[`Policy I only`,`Policy II only`,`Policy III only`,`Policy I and Policy III only`],"B",`Policy II has two expansionary moves (more money, more disposable income). Policy I has one of each and Policy III has one of each (a higher discount rate is contractionary), so they are mixed.`);
Qs("D49","D","HKDSE 2020-I Q.35",38,`Which of the following policies would offset the effect of an increase in the willingness to invest on the price level? (1) an increase in profits tax rate (2) an increase in transfer payment (3) a decrease in tax allowance`,[`(1) and (2) only`,`(1) and (3) only`,`(2) and (3) only`,`(1), (2) and (3)`],"B",`More willingness to invest raises AD and the price level. To offset, AD must fall: a higher profits tax (1) and a smaller allowance (3). More transfers (2) raise AD.`);
Qs("D50","D","HKDSE 2020-I Q.36",38,`The economy operates at E0 (a deflationary gap; imports equal exports). If the government decreases social security assistance for the elderly, in the short run the deflationary (output) gap will become ______ and there will be a ______.`,[`narrower ... trade surplus`,`narrower ... trade deficit`,`wider ... trade surplus`,`wider ... trade deficit`],"C",`Same reasoning as D41: AD falls, output falls further (a wider gap), imports fall: a trade surplus.`,{c:[`AS-AD diagram with E0 to the left of LRAS. See the page.`]});
Qs("D51","D","HKDSE 2020-I Q.38",38,`In 2019, the Japanese government increases the general sales tax rate from 8% to 10%. As a result, (1) income inequality will be higher. (2) the fiscal deficit will be eliminated. (3) the invisible trade deficit will decrease.`,[`(1) only`,`(2) only`,`(3) only`,`(1), (2) and (3)`],"A",`A regressive tax widens inequality (1). A bigger sales tax does not eliminate a deficit (2), and the effect on invisible trade is not clear (3).`);
Qs("D52","D","HKDSE 2020-I Q.41",39,`Without tax exemption John pays $15,000 and Mary $35,000 of income tax. Measure I exempts 75% of income tax up to $30,000; Measure II exempts 100% of income tax up to $20,000. John will prefer ______ while Mary will prefer ______.`,[`measure I ... measure I`,`measure I ... measure II`,`measure II ... measure I`,`measure II ... measure II`],"C",T`John: Measure I saves @@0.75\times15000=11250@@; Measure II saves @@\min(15000,20000)=15000@@, so John prefers II. Mary: Measure I saves @@\min(0.75\times35000,30000)=26250@@; Measure II saves @@20000@@, so Mary prefers I.`);

const LONG_D=[
{id:"LD1",src:`HKCEE 1997-I Q.9d (printed answer p.60)`,p:39,pr:"39",f:"s6",k:["s6",60,"60"],q:`A general sales tax is introduced to finance the construction of the new railway. Explain the effect of this tax on Hong Kong's (i) price level (2 marks) (ii) income distribution (3 marks).`,m:`<p>(i) The prices of goods and services increase because the cost of production rises, so the price level rises.</p><p>(ii) The low-income group and the high-income group pay the same amount of tax when they buy the same goods, so the low-income group pays a <b>larger proportion of their income</b>. The income distribution becomes <b>more uneven</b>.</p>`},
{id:"LD2",src:`HKCEE 2000-I Q.8 (printed answer p.60)`,p:39,pr:"39",f:"s6",k:["s6",60,"60"],q:`In 1999 the government refunded 10% of the salaries tax and profits tax to taxpayers. Explain how this would affect the wealth gap between the low-income group and the high-income group. (4 marks)`,m:`<p>The refund <b>widens</b> the gap. The salaries tax is progressive, so the high-income group is refunded more (in dollars and proportion) than the low-income group, while the poorer non-taxpayers receive nothing.</p>`},
{id:"LD3",src:`HKCEE 2000-I Q.9a ii (printed answer p.60)`,p:39,pr:"39",f:"s6",k:["s6",60,"60"],q:`Give TWO economic reasons for the government to levy tax on gasoline. (4 marks)`,m:`<p>Any two: (1) Gasoline is a <b>necessity</b>, so the tax broadens the tax base and raises revenue. (2) Taxing gasoline <b>discourages</b> private car use and encourages public transport, reducing air pollution and congestion. (3) The government can control the price and use of gasoline and promote greener energy.</p>`},
{id:"LD4",src:`HKCEE 2001-I Q.7b (printed answer p.60)`,p:39,pr:"39",f:"s6",k:["s6",60,"60"],q:`Explain the effect of a general sales tax on Hong Kong's income distribution. (3 marks)`,m:`<p>Both groups pay the same amount of tax when they buy the same goods, so the low-income group pays a larger proportion of its income. The income distribution becomes <b>more uneven</b>.</p>`},
{id:"LD5",src:`HKCEE 2006-I Q.11b (printed answer p.60)`,p:40,pr:"40",f:"s6",k:["s6",60,"60"],q:`'The tax base is narrow' and 'the government revenue is unstable'. Suppose a general sales tax is introduced. Explain its effect on the tax base. (2 marks)`,m:`<p>Assuming salaries, employment, profit and consumption are not greatly hurt, a general sales tax <b>broadens the tax base</b>: most goods and services were untaxed before but will be taxed. The government receives tax in addition to existing sources.</p>`}
];

/* ---- Section E: government budget (Ch.6 paper pages 44-49, key p.70). Question 10 is marked "Del." (deleted) in the key and is omitted. ---- */
Qs("E1","E","HKCEE 1997-II Q.46",44,`The Hong Kong government spends $2 billion in the construction of a new highway which is financed by its fiscal reserve. Which of the following would MOST likely occur?`,[`a narrower wealth gap between the rich and the poor`,`an increase in the employment rate`,`an increase in the inflation rate`,`a budget deficit in the years of construction`],"B",`Construction spending raises AD and creates jobs. Financing from reserves means there is no deficit (D false); a one-off rise in prices is not inflation (C false); there is no reason for the wealth gap to narrow (A).`);
Qs("E2","E","HKCEE 1999-II Q.41",44,`A surplus budget could turn out to be a budgetary deficit if the actual government revenue is ______ than the actual government expenditure and the estimated government revenue is ______ than the estimated government expenditure.`,[`greater ... greater`,`smaller ... greater`,`greater ... smaller`,`smaller ... smaller`],"B",`A surplus budget is a plan: estimated revenue is greater than estimated expenditure. It turns out as a deficit if the actual revenue falls below actual expenditure.`);
Qs("E3","E","HKCEE 2000-II Q.45",44,`The government will participate in the project for establishing Disneyland in Hong Kong. During the period of construction, it must`,[`increase the tax burden on taxpayers.`,`increase the size of the public sector.`,`increase the ratio of public expenditure to GDP.`,`discourage the incentive of private investment.`],"B",`The government spends more, so the public sector (the amount of resources it uses) grows. The ratio of expenditure to GDP need not rise (C): GDP rises too. It need not raise taxes, and the project may even encourage private investment.`);
Qs("E4","E","HKCEE 2001-II Q.37",45,`Charts show the General Revenue Account composition in 1998 (direct taxes 42%, indirect taxes 30%, other revenues 28%) and 1999 (direct 46%, indirect 22%, other 32%). Based on the data, in the year 1999`,[`the ratio of direct taxes to indirect taxes increased.`,`the total amount of tax revenue increased.`,`the proportion of tax revenue in total government revenue increased.`,`government revenue from rates decreased by 8%.`],"A",T`Ratio direct:indirect: 1998 @@42/30=1.4@@, 1999 @@46/22\approx2.1@@: it rose. The pie charts give shares, not amounts, so B and D cannot be known; the tax share fell from 72% to 68% (C false).`,{c:[`Two pie charts for 1998 and 1999 with the percentages above. See the page.`]});
Qs("E5","E","HKCEE 2001-II Q.38",45,`If Hong Kong is in the stage of recovery in the business cycle,`,[`government expenditure will increase.`,`the proportion of tax revenue in total government revenue will decrease.`,`government revenue from direct taxes will increase.`,`there will be a budgetary surplus for the government.`],"C",`Recovery raises incomes and profits, so salaries tax and profits tax revenue rise. Expenditure and the budget balance need not change in a definite way.`);
Qs("E6","E","HKCEE 2002-II Q.41",45,`Government expenditure grows at a constant 10% a year while the growth rate of GDP is below 10% and slowly rising. According to the graph, the ratio of G to GDP is`,[`increasing.`,`decreasing.`,`first increasing and then decreasing.`,`first decreasing and then increasing.`],"A",T`G grows faster than GDP in every year (10% vs a rate below 10%), so @@G/GDP@@ keeps rising.`,{c:[`Growth rate against year: G is a horizontal line at 10%; GDP growth starts near 4% and rises slowly but stays below 10%. See the page.`]});
Qs("E7","E","HKCEE 2010-II Q.44",46,`Which of the following will increase in times of economic recovery?`,[`trade surplus`,`budget deficit`,`government expenditure`,`direct tax revenue`],"D",`Rising incomes and profits raise direct tax revenue. A recovery tends to raise imports (trade surplus falls) and to improve the budget.`);
Qs("E8","E","HKDSE 2012-I Q.35",46,`Charts show the General Revenue Account composition in 2008-09 (direct tax 46.2%, indirect tax 22.8%, other revenues 31.0%) and 2009-10 (direct tax 38.7%, indirect tax 26.6%, other revenues 34.7%). Based on the data, in the year 2009-2010`,[`there was an economic recession in Hong Kong.`,`the ratio of direct taxes to indirect taxes increased.`,`the total amount of tax revenue decreased.`,`the proportion of tax revenue in total government revenue decreased.`],"D",T`Tax share: @@46.2+22.8=69.0\%@@ became @@38.7+26.6=65.3\%@@, so the proportion fell. The direct:indirect ratio fell (A: 2.03 to 1.45, so B false). Amounts and the recession cannot be read from the pies.`,{c:[`Two pie charts for 2008-2009 and 2009-2010 with the percentages above. See the page.`]});
Qs("E9","E","HKDSE 2013-I Q.31",46,`Initially an economy operates at the full-employment equilibrium and the government expenditure equals its tax revenue. Which of the following will result in a coexistence of deflationary (output) gap and budget deficit?`,[`a reduction in taxes`,`a reduction in money supply`,`a reduction in government expenditure`,`a reduction in desire to import`],"B",`A lower money supply raises interest rates, cuts AD (a deflationary gap) and so cuts tax revenue (a deficit as G is unchanged). A tax cut raises AD; lower G lowers the deficit; a lower desire to import raises AD.`);
Qs("E11","E","HKDSE 2016-I Q.40",47,`Under which of the following situations may a fiscal deficit and a trade deficit be eliminated at the same time? (1) a decrease in nominal interest rate (2) a depreciation of the economy's currency (3) an increase in income tax`,[`(1) and (2) only`,`(1) and (3) only`,`(2) and (3) only`,`(1), (2) and (3)`],"C",`(2) cheaper exports and dearer imports raise NX and income (so tax revenue), cutting both deficits. (3) raises revenue directly and lowers imports through lower income. (1) raises AD and imports, so it would worsen the trade balance.`);
Qs("E12","E","HKDSE 2018-I Q.36",47,`A deflationary (output) gap and a fiscal deficit exist. Which of the following would narrow the deflationary gap and reduce the fiscal deficit at the same time?`,[`increasing government expenditure on infrastructure`,`reducing the profits tax rate`,`increasing the tax rebate on salaries tax`,`allowing more tourists to travel to the country visa-free`],"D",`More tourists raise exports of services: AD rises (a narrower gap) and the extra income raises tax revenue without extra spending, so the deficit falls. A, B, C raise AD but cost revenue or spending, which does not clearly reduce the deficit.`);
Qs("E13","E","HKDSE 2019-I Q.40",47,`An inflationary (output) gap and a fiscal surplus exist. Which of the following measures would narrow the inflationary gap and attain a balanced fiscal condition at the same time?`,[`reducing quota for inbound tourists`,`raising income tax rate`,`adopting a universal retirement protection scheme for all elderly aged 65 or above`,`increasing government spending on infrastructure`],"A",`Fewer tourists lower AD (a smaller inflationary gap) and lower income, so tax revenue falls, moving the surplus toward balance. Raising income tax narrows the gap but increases the surplus; C and D raise AD.`);

const LONG_E=[
{id:"LE1",src:`HKCEE 1997-I Q.7 (printed answer p.70)`,p:47,pr:"47",f:"s6",k:["s6",70,"70"],q:`A pie chart shows sources of government revenue in Hong Kong: indirect taxes, other revenue, and a section X. (a) Name X. (1) (b) Explain why the government revenue from X is unstable. (4)`,m:`<p>(a) X is <b>direct taxes</b>. (b) Direct taxes such as salaries tax and profits tax vary with the income of workers and firms, which changes with economic conditions. So the direct tax revenue also fluctuates with economic conditions.</p>`},
{id:"LE2",src:`HKCEE 1998-I Q.7b (printed answer p.70)`,p:48,pr:"48",f:"s6",k:["s6",70,"70"],q:`The salaries tax allowance in Hong Kong remains unchanged. Explain why the total tax revenue from this tax would increase at times of inflation. (3 marks)`,m:`<p>During inflation the <b>nominal income</b> of people increases. With the same allowance, more people are put into the tax net or into higher tax brackets, so the salaries tax revenue increases.</p>`},
{id:"LE3",src:`HKCEE 2003-I Q.8 (printed answer p.70)`,p:48,pr:"48",f:"s6",k:["s6",70,"70"],q:`Give TWO reasons to explain why the direct tax revenue of the Hong Kong government would decrease in times of economic recession. (4 marks)`,m:`<p>Any two: (1) workers' incomes fall and some lose their jobs, so <b>salaries tax</b> falls. (2) Firms earn less profit and some close, so <b>profits tax</b> falls. (3) Landlords' rental income falls, so <b>property tax</b> falls. (4) The value of assets falls, so estate duty falls (when it existed).</p>`},
{id:"LE4",src:`HKCEE 2004-I Q.9a (printed answer p.70)`,p:48,pr:"48",f:"s6",k:["s6",70,"70"],q:`Hong Kong is facing a huge fiscal deficit and deflation. Explain how government expenditure and revenue are affected by Hong Kong's (i) economic downturn (4) and (ii) ageing population (4).`,m:`<p>(i) <b>Downturn:</b> tax revenue falls as workers' income, firms' profits and landlords' rent fall. The government spends more on social welfare and on programmes to stimulate employment and growth. (ii) <b>Ageing:</b> more elderly people need health care and welfare, so expenditure rises; fewer people of working age pay salaries tax, so revenue falls.</p>`},
{id:"LE6",src:`HKCEE 2006-I Q.11b (ii)`,p:48,pr:"48",f:"s6",k:["s6",70,"70"],q:`Explain how the government revenue generated from profits tax and salaries tax would be affected when there is a strong economic recovery. (4 marks)`,m:`<p>Incomes and employment rise, so salaries tax revenue rises (more people enter the net, some move into higher brackets). Firms' profits rise, so profits tax revenue rises. So revenue from both taxes increases.</p>`},
{id:"LE8",src:`HKCEE 2009-I Q.11a`,p:49,pr:"49",f:"s6",k:["s6",70,"70"],q:`The corporation profits tax rate was reduced from 17.5% to 16.5%. Explain whether the government revenue from profits tax would necessarily decrease as a result. (4 marks)`,m:T`<p><b>Not necessarily.</b> @@\text{revenue}=\text{rate}\times\text{profit}@@. The lower rate raises after-tax profit and the incentive to invest, which can raise profits. If profits rise by a larger percentage than the rate falls, revenue rises.</p>`},
{id:"LE9",src:`HKDSE 2013-II Q.8`,p:49,pr:"49",f:"s6",k:["s6",70,"70"],q:`A government is facing a budget deficit. Some politicians propose raising the income tax rate to reduce the deficit. Evaluate their proposal. (4 marks)`,m:`<p><b>For:</b> a higher rate raises revenue per dollar of income, which can narrow the deficit and a progressive rise narrows the income gap. <b>Against:</b> it lowers disposable income and so consumption (AD falls) and the incentive to work, which can lower income and tax revenue, so the effect on the deficit is uncertain. Weigh the two and reach a conclusion.</p>`}
];

/* ---- merged Ch.6 sections END ---- */
const EX=[
{id:"F1",k:"num",t:`Tell the tax type from the data`,stem:T`Tax payments are $1,000 on taxable income of $10,000; $1,900 on $20,000; $2,700 on $30,000. What is the <b>average tax rate</b> (%) at $30,000? (HKCEE style, printed answer p.57)`,a:9,u:`percent`,tol:0.01,src:[["s6",57,"57"]],w:T`@@\dfrac{2700}{30000}\times100\%=9\%@@. The rates are 10%, 9.5%, 9%: falling, so the tax is <b>regressive</b>. (Printed answer: "No, the average tax rate does not increase as income increases".)`},
{id:"F2",k:"open",t:`Raising the allowance`,stem:`The government raises the salaries tax allowance. State its effect on the income gap, the tax base and the number of people paying at the standard rate.`,src:[S(4,"5")],w:`<p><b>Income gap:</b> narrows, because fewer low-income people pay tax. <b>Tax base:</b> narrows, because fewer people fall into the tax net. <b>People paying at the standard rate:</b> decreases, because under the progressive schedule people pay less.</p>`},
{id:"F3",k:"open",t:`Revenue stability`,stem:`Explain why indirect tax revenue is more stable than direct tax revenue over the business cycle.`,src:[S(6,"8")],w:`<p>Indirect taxes are mainly levied on goods and services. Most people want a stable living standard (consumption smoothing), so consumption does not fluctuate much over the cycle. Incomes, which direct taxes are levied on, fluctuate a lot. So revenue from consumption is more stable than revenue from incomes.</p>`}
];

const LONG=[
{id:"E1",src:`HKCEE 2002-I Q.8 (printed answer p.56)`,p:2,pr:"2",f:"s6",q:`State and explain TWO principles of taxation put forward by Adam Smith. (4 marks)`,m:`<p>Any two:</p><ul><li><b>Equity:</b> the tax payment should be in direct proportion to the taxpayers' income.</li><li><b>Certainty:</b> the government must clearly define the responsibilities of taxpayers, who should be clearly informed of calculation methods, deadlines and modes of payment.</li><li><b>Convenience:</b> deadlines and methods of payment should be convenient to taxpayers.</li><li><b>Economy:</b> the administrative costs of collection should be kept to a minimum.</li></ul>`,k:["s6",56,"56"]},
{id:"E2",src:`HKCEE 1999-I Q.10c (printed answer p.59)`,p:2,pr:"2",f:"s6",q:`Mr. Ng is a Hong Kong citizen. He sets up a firm in Mainland China and earns profits. (i) Does he pay profits tax in Hong Kong? (ii) He uses Hong Kong International Airport when flying to the Mainland. Which tax is involved?`,m:`<p>(i) No: his profits are earned in Mainland China, not in Hong Kong (territorial source principle). (ii) The air passenger departure tax.</p>`,k:["s6",59,"59"]},
{id:"E3",src:`Practice (framework from the notes)`,q:`Under proposal B the tax payment takes an increasing proportion of taxable income as income rises. Explain its effect on the income gap and how a government can broaden the tax base.`,m:T`<p>Proposal B is a <b>progressive</b> tax: high-income people pay a larger proportion of their income and low-income people a smaller one, so it <b>narrows</b> the income gap.</p><p><b>Broadening the base:</b> reduce the tax allowance (more people fall into the tax net) or introduce a new type of tax (such as a general sales tax), so more people and goods are taxed and revenue is more stable.</p>`}
];

const CS=[
{t:`Common mistakes with fiscal policy and public finance`,zh:`常犯錯誤`,src:[S(10,"16"),S(10,"17"),S(11,"18")],h:`
<div class="tw"><table>
<tr><th>Topic</th><th>Typical mistakes</th></tr>
<tr><td>Direct versus indirect</td><td>Knowing the definition but not the exact meaning of "shifting the tax burden" or to whom it is shifted</td></tr>
<tr><td>The tax system in Hong Kong</td><td>Answering what tax a person pays when the question asks which <i>direct</i> tax · thinking Hong Kong has a general sales tax · mixing rates (on ownership, by estimated rental value, paid by the owner) with property tax (on rental <i>income</i>) · thinking shareholders pay profits tax (it is the company, a separate legal entity) while shareholders owe dividend tax (if any)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Past (abolished) taxes</td><td>Not knowing some taxes were abolished; some past questions are out of date, so practise carefully</td></tr>
<tr><td>Properties of proportional, regressive and progressive taxes</td><td>Defining progressive as "pay more when income rises" (it is "pay a <b>higher proportion</b> of income as tax") · not seeing that a rising tax <i>rate</i> means tax payment grows faster than income · confusing the graphs (tax payment: slope; tax rate: direction) · forgetting the horizontal axis is taxable income, not gross income</td></tr>
<tr><td>Identifying the type of tax</td><td>Thinking a fixed percentage means proportional: it depends on <i>of what</i> the percentage is taken</td></tr>
<tr><td>Principles of taxation</td><td>Mixing convenience (how to pay, for example online) with certainty (the obligation is clearly defined and publicly announced)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Changes in salaries tax</td><td>Not knowing a higher allowance spreads income more evenly · not knowing progressivity can be raised by increasing marginal rates or narrowing bands · mixing up tax base (people and goods in the net) · assuming a higher tax rate always raises revenue (the work-incentive effect makes it uncertain) · not using the diagram to count people paying at the standard rate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inflation and tax brackets</td><td>Not using the diagram · not knowing nominal incomes rise in inflation · not realising progressive taxes make the percentage change in tax larger than that in income</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stability of revenue</td><td>Not saying which type of revenue or spending the cycle changes · forgetting consumption smoothing · not knowing the conditions for broadening the tax base (a new tax or a lower allowance)</td></tr>
<tr><td>General sales tax</td><td>Thinking a sales tax causes sustained inflation (the price rise is a one-off) · thinking it mainly shifts AD (it mainly shifts SRAS through cost)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fiscal balance and the public sector</td><td>Not starting from the type of tax or spending · not seeing that growth in public expenditure versus growth in GDP decides the direction of the public sector's size</td></tr>
<tr><td>The budget</td><td>Mixing deficit budget (the planned policy, decided in advance) with budget deficit (the result at the end of the year)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inequality</td><td>Not naming which people earn more and for what reason · missing key concepts (equalising income and opportunity) · not seeing opportunity means the poor are less hindered by background · missing "regressive" and "different people pay the same amount of tax for the same good" · not reading a Lorenz point: the poorest x% earn y%</td></tr>
</table></div>`}
];

